### Page 15 of 26

```text
15

Project Approach
Our proposed approach is described in the following tasks and is based on the requirements of the RFP
and successful completion of similar work in other communities.
TASK 1: PROJECT INITIATION & PROJECT MANAGEMENT
Project administration and coordination will be ongoing throughout the study development process as
our team works closely with the Cheyenne MPO and CTP staff. We approach the study acutely aware of
the need to incorporate the appropriate agencies, individuals, and representatives—we view our role as
an extension of the local MPO and City staff.
We will work with Cheyenne MPO and CTP staff to establish a Project Steering Committee (PSC) which
will be responsible for giving feedback on project development and deliverables. We will meet with the
PSC on a regular basis, whether in person, by phone, by email, or by web conference, to ensure good
communication on upcoming tasks and to make sure the project remains on time and within budget. We
will meet with the MPO Citizens’ Advisory Committee, Technical Committee, and Transit Advisory Board
at key points during the study process to present summaries of progress, to obtain feedback, and to gain
input for direction of the plan. We will coordinate these meetings through the PSC.
We will begin by scheduling the kick-off meeting and preparing a data-needs list. Following the kick-off
meeting we will prepare a Project Management Plan, with the final scope of work, project schedule, and
community outreach, which will guide completion of the TDP. We will develop lines of communication
and provide routine updates to ensure that local staff are well aware of our efforts and progress. We will
provide monthly invoices detailing staff hours and expenses incurred with a project summary describing
the work completed during the previous month, situations that could impact the schedule, and
anticipated work for the following month. We will be in contact at key points to discuss any issues and
to coordinate upcoming activities. This approach ensures that staff are informed of study progress and
any challenges encountered.
Task 1 Deliverables:
 Kickoff meeting and community familiarization
 Project Management Plan with final scope of work, schedule, and community outreach plan
 Monthly meetings with the PSC coordinated with particular deliverables and project activities
(see Project Schedule)
 Meeting materials, agendas, and notes
TASK 2: REVIEW OF EXISTING DOCUMENTS & COMMUNITY CONDITIONS
A key step in our approach is developing a deep understanding of current community conditions and
transit needs in order to understand how the community has historically been thinking about
transportation needs, priorities, and strategies.
Review of Existing Documents
We will review all pertinent long-term and short-term plans that may have transportation aspects
including local comprehensive plans, Cheyenne MPO plans, economic development plans, bicycle and
```

### Page 16 of 26

```text
16

pedestrian plans, and previous transit plans. We will summarize and present relevant existing
documents in annotated form and identify documents that include supportive transit elements.
Analysis of Community Conditions
Our team has experienced staff that are well versed in analyzing community demographic data, housing
and socioeconomic data, land use patterns, and commuter-travel-pattern data using the most recent
U.S. Census, American Community Survey (ACS), and other applicable data sources. As part of this task,
our team will also review and analyze existing safety and transit-performance targets and measures.
In addition to the general population, demographic data will be analyzed for individuals considered by
the transportation profession to be dependent upon public transit, including: ambulatory disabled
population, youth population, older adult population, low-income population, and population with
limited automobile availability. Maps will be prepared showing the characteristics of the transit-
dependent population by number, percentage, and density for each US Census block group. From this
demographic analysis, as well as input from the PSC, drivers, and the community, we will catalogue and
characterize local activity centers and possible transit trip generators in the study area to create an
understanding of existing and potential transit use.
Task 2 Deliverables:
 The results and analysis of Task 2 will be included in Interim Report #1.
TASK 3: PUBLIC INVOLVEMENT
Public outreach should be a fluid sharing of information, ideas, concerns, and solutions between the
agency and the community throughout the duration of the study. To achieve this on this project, we will
employ various innovative communication tools to access a variety of forums providing multiple
opportunities for the community to learn and voice opinions. However, we don’t just inform the public
but rather engage them. Using our approach, community engagement is not a separate set of tasks
but rather an integral part of the full process to develop a recommended service plan.
Facilitating meaningful community conversations is a key function of any project, and an area where our
team stands out and excels by implementing innovative and inclusive public engagement strategies. To
engage residents and key stakeholders in a meaningful way, we will employ a robust public engagement
process that goes beyond traditional engagement tools and employs a combination of strategies
designed to equitably engage all audiences.
Our team recognizes that many people in the Cheyenne community speak Spanish as their primary
language. We are committed to meeting the needs of these community members by:
•
Ensuring outreach events are located in different neighborhoods, so as to reduce physical
barriers to attendance.
•
Advertising events and engagement opportunities through a variety of mediums and in
English and Spanish.
•
Reaching out to multilingual community groups.
•
Providing staff supported Spanish translation and interpretation services for community
meetings and open houses.
Through our engagement work, we know that you cannot simply translate outreach materials to Spanish
to effectively engage the Latino community. We have found Facebook Live, the Spanish radio stations,
and community events to be effective ways to engage in conversations about transportation challenges.
```

### Page 17 of 26

```text
17

Anticipated engagement methods tailored to fit the needs of the planning process are described below.
Community Survey
An online community transportation survey (in English and Spanish) will be performed to gauge the
public perception of current and future transportation needs, gaps, and demand in the Cheyenne area,
as well as gain insights on the public’s thoughts and opinions of existing CTP service. While many people
will not participate in public meetings, they may take the time to complete a short online questionnaire
at a time that is convenient for them, particularly during COVID-19. However, we understand that there
will be residents who cannot or choose not to use the online version so a PDF version will also be made
available. The survey link will be posted on the project website, and we will work with the PSC to have it
incorporated in newsletters, distributed by local stakeholders, and shared on social media. We will
tabulate responses and provide a summary report.
Onboard Survey
Our team proposes to conduct an onboard survey of fixed-route transit riders. However, we recognize
that CTP is currently operating a curbside on-demand service, so we will work with the PSC at the kick-
off meeting to determine how to best conduct this survey if services remain on-demand or if fixed-route
service resumes. An onboard survey is the best means of engaging existing transit riders in the planning
process. Many passengers are willing to complete a survey questionnaire during their bus trip, while the
number of passengers who typically attend any type of meeting is much lower. The trip origins and
destinations and the level of satisfaction with existing CTP services will provide our team and the PSC
with insight into the most appropriate transportation services that will maximize system utilization and
operational efficiency. A self-administered bilingual (English/Spanish) survey questionnaire will be used
to collect the data, and have been used by the LSC team in hundreds of onboard surveys.
Project Website and Promotional Materials
Our team will develop a study website with interactive tools to allow for community input and feedback.
All of the online interaction will take place through the study website, including information about the
study, project schedule, and upcoming engagement activities. Links to the online survey and comment
forms will also be included on the project website. We will use the various outreach efforts to develop
an email list of interested individuals and organizations and will use that list to inform community
members when new information is posted or when there is an opportunity to participate in the study.
We will also provide all necessary project information and electronic materials to the MPO and City for
posting on their existing websites.
Promotional materials such as handouts, postcards, and posters can provide basic project information
and recruit a variety of audiences to participate in upcoming engagement opportunities. We will
develop these materials at key points in the project that describe the project, announce public meetings,
drive survey participation, and share important project updates. Handouts and postcards may be
distributed at meetings to share project information, while posters may be posted in nearby businesses
to announce meetings and engagement activities. Electronic versions can be emailed, posted on the
MPO/City websites, shared on social media, and incorporated into existing publications and newsletters.
Community Meetings
We propose to hold community meetings at two key points in the planning process. Community
meetings will be held in different areas throughout Cheyenne to provide opportunity for the entire
community to participate in this planning process. In addition, a Spanish speaking member of our team
will be on site to assist with engagement.
```

### Page 18 of 26

```text
18

The purpose of the first meeting is to inform the community about the project and findings to-date,
obtain preliminary input on the issues and opportunities for improvements, and gather feedback on the
community’s vision for future transit in Cheyenne. The purpose of the second meeting is to present
initial transit service scenarios to the community and gather specific feedback on preferences, balanced
against funding constraints. At this time, we will also prepare an online comment form requesting
specific input from the public, including their evaluation of the transit service options presented and
their level of support for, or opposition to, each of the transit service options. This will provide an
opportunity for those unable to attend the community meeting to provide feedback. The input gained
from the community transit service options workshop and the online comment form will help determine
the recommended service plan.
Task 3 Deliverables:
 Community survey
 Onboard survey
 Project website and promotional materials
 Community meetings, meeting materials (displays, handouts, sign-in sheets), and meeting
summaries
 Spanish translation services (surveys, outreach materials, meetings)
TASK 4: EVALUATION OF EXISTING SERVICE & RIDERSHIP
To create the foundation for reimagining the CTP system and preparing the TDP, our team will perform a
thorough evaluation of current and pre-pandemic services using strategies including:
System, Service Type, and Route Performance
COVID-19 has impacted how all transit agencies operate and many, like CTP, have shifted from fixed-
route service to other service delivery models in order to leverage vehicles and improve performance.
For this analysis, it will be important to evaluate pre-pandemic fixed-route service (systemwide and by
individual route) and ADA paratransit service, as well as the current on-demand service. Data to be
analyzed will include:
•
Hours of operation and level of service
•
Passenger ridership data; trip origin/destination, patterns, and purposes
•
Performance measures
•
Existing fleet size, condition, and use
•
Operational infrastructure usage and performance
•
Financials, including fares
From these data, we will develop route profiles showing the route alignment, passenger characteristics,
and performance measures. For each route, on-time performance, passengers per hour, cost per
passenger, route connectivity, populations served, and primary origin-destination information will be
reported.
Development of a Cost Allocation Model
Our team will create a cost-structure methodology for existing services, under the current operating
model, based on financial reports and established budgets. This cost allocation model will quantify fixed
and variable costs and will serve as a valuable tool for determining cost of adding or reducing services
under various service development models.
```

### Page 19 of 26

```text
19

Peer Comparison
As part of this Task, we will conduct a peer comparison of similar transit agencies in terms of existing
service costs, delivery models, efficiency, and effectiveness. Our team will identify 5-10 similar peers
to CTP, using data from the National Transit Database (NTD), the Wyoming Department of
Transportation (WyDOT), and other sources as available. The selection of peer communities will be
based on input from the PSC.
Review Existing Service Standards
We will review the current service standards to understand whether they reflect the stated
organizational goals and if they provide helpful feedback to management. We will compare stated
service standards to actual service operated to understand whether updates are needed. In evaluating
and suggesting potential new service standards, we will create options for standards, based on ability to
measure with accurate data, ease of ongoing tracking, and potential to provide more meaningful
standards that measure the impact of the service on the mobility of the community.
Inventory of Existing Amenities
Our team will do an on-the-ground review of the existing transit amenities that help provide a safer and
more comfortable passenger experience. Using a “new user” perspective, we will understand how easy
it is to use and understand the current system, from finding bus stops, navigating the system, and
waiting for the bus. How comfortable and safe do new passengers feel? How well are stops signed?
Where are the opportunities for improvements in transit amenities? We will ask these questions as we
review the state of the current system.
The existing fleet will be reviewed with regard to size, age, and replacement schedule. Ridership levels
will be examined to assess the appropriate vehicle capacity for each route. Existing operating,
maintenance, and passenger facilities will be reviewed and compared with forecast requirements to
define needed improvements and expansions.
Task 4 Deliverables:
 The results and analysis of Task 4 will be included in Interim Report #1.
TASK 5: EVALUATION OF NEEDED CHANGES OR EXPANSION IN SERVICE &
AMENITIES
Transit Needs and Demand Assessment
LSC has performed research for the Transit Cooperative Research Program in transit demand estimation.
We have staff who have worked for years with travel models and have experience developing
quantitative estimates of transportation needs and potential demand. We will develop a fixed-route
transit demand model calibrated to the pre-pandemic service and ridership. We will use this model in
conjunction with ACS data to develop estimates of demand for unserved areas and areas of possible
future development. A calibrated transit demand model is needed to determine the level of demand for
proposed route changes or service to new areas. We will also develop a transit demand model for the
demand-response service to evaluate potential changes in the complementary paratransit and demand-
response service which might result from proposed service changes. These quantitative estimates will
be combined with the more qualitative statements of need obtained through input from riders and the
public to gain a comprehensive picture of existing unmet and future transit needs.
```

### Page 20 of 26

```text
20

First and Last Mile Gap Analysis
Fehr & Peers has extensive experience incorporating access to transit and multimodal connectivity into
our transit planning efforts. A seamless user experience across multimodal transportation modes with
transit at the center of the experience is the goal. We will review the current bicycle and pedestrian
network and infrastructure to assess strengths and define multimodal opportunities and strategies for
the TDP. We will also examine opportunities for better integration with potential Transportation
Network Companies (TNCs), such as Uber and Lyft, depending on service availability. The new Cheyenne
microtransit program will also be assessed for seamless integration as a first- and last-mile solution for
the fixed-route system. Other community partnerships and possible technology solutions will be
evaluated for deployment as ways to mitigate service gaps.
Task 5 Deliverables:
 The results and analysis of Task 5 will be included in Interim Report #1.
TASK 6: DEVELOPMENT OF RECOMMENDED SERVICE PLANS
 Our team approaches transit service planning with no preconceptions and with a “fresh approach” to
how a transit service may be operated. Service scenarios will be developed to meet the needs of the
community, as identified through the data collection, current and pre-pandemic service analysis, and
outreach processes described in previous tasks.
The preliminary service scenarios will incorporate various service concepts and alternative service
delivery models (building on CTP’s existing success with microtransit). These options will be configured
within the existing hours of service with opportunities identified for growth, should funding become
available. We will also take into account the future timeline for implementation of potential services
(within 0-to-2-years, 2-to-5-years, and 5-to-10-years). Throughout the development of the service
scenarios, input from the public and riders will be incorporated. All service alternatives will be evaluated
against strategic questions about tradeoffs and considerations, including:
•
What is the overall service philosophy: geographic-coverage system, high-frequency
intensive system, lifeline system for the transit dependent, or commuter-focused?
•
What are the efficiency tradeoffs in terms of ridership, costs, and travel times?
•
What is the estimated relative performance of corridors in areas of ridership demand,
costs, and safety?
We will present the initial scenarios in Interim Report #2 and at meetings to the PSC and the public for
input and feedback. Based on the feedback received, we will refine the concepts and develop the
recommended service plans, including routing structure and bus stops, timetables, service hours and
operating details, ridership forecasts, performance metrics, cost estimates, and any potential policy
changes needed for implementation. The recommended service plans will be presented in Interim
Report #3.
Task 6 Deliverables:
 Initial service alternatives will be presented in Interim Report #2 to allow for feedback from the
PSC and the public.
 Interim Report #3 will include the recommended service plans.
```

### Page 21 of 26

```text
21

TASK 7 – 10-YEAR CAPITAL ASSET PLAN
To complement the service plans and provide a compliant Transit Asset Management (TAM) Plan, a
complete ten-year Capital Improvement Plan will be developed to reflect projected investments in the
Cheyenne Transit Program fleet, bus stops, technology, facilities, park-and-ride lots, and supporting on-
street infrastructure. The plan will be structured with a timeline that will show how each project,
especially fleet replacements, will need to occur in order to maintain a state of good repair. The impact
of the service plans, and potential for more of a shift to microtransit, will be analyzed and included in
the ten-year investments.
Our team will generate a range of funding and financing options matched to specific capital projects,
according to applicability. In addition to local and state funding resources, the team will evaluate federal
sources, such as the Federal Transit Administration’s (FTA) Small Starts, New Starts, and Low/No
Emission Bus Grants, and the U.S. Department of Transportation’s Better Utilizing Investments to
Leverage Development (BUILD Transportation Discretionary Grant).
Task 7 Deliverables:
 The Capital Asset Plan will be included in Interim Report #3.
TASK 8: PERFORMANCE INDICATORS & TARGETS
Perhaps as vital to the long-term success of the TDP’s vision is the monitoring program set up to track
the progress of the various new and existing services, and to “flag” potential problems as they occur.
This monitoring program will identify the data collection and analysis of established performance
measures, as well as define new measures and associated benchmarks. The measures should have
acceptable ranges, based on similar communities (using the same peer communities included in the
peer comparison in Task 5), goals, targets, and considerations for how to make adjustments when
particular metrics fall below goals. LSC has a depth of experience developing these performance
measures and creating systems that allow for easy, ongoing management and updating.
Task 8 Deliverables:
 Interim Report #3 will include the performance indicators and targets.
TASK 9: FINANCIAL PLAN
Our team will develop a 10-year financial plan for the recommended service plans, including operations
and capital, using constant dollars and accounting for anticipated inflation and possible changes in
funding. The plan will be built around existing resources and will identify potential savings over the
current budget or potential funding shortfalls and required revenue to meet the projected expenses. We
will also identify potential funding sources including local, state, and federal funding, as well as other
grant opportunities.
Task 9 Deliverables:
 The Financial Plan will be included in Interim Report #3.
TASK 10: DRAFT AND FINAL REPORTS
Prepare Draft Report
The Draft Report will synthesize the work of the interim reports and project deliverables, as well as
address input or comments received on them. The Draft Report will be a comprehensive documentation
of the study process, methodologies, findings, and recommendations. The PSC, stakeholders, the public,
```

### Page 22 of 26

```text
22

and other parties identified by the MPO and CTP staff will all have the opportunity to review the Draft
Report and provide feedback.
Prepare and Present Final Report
Following review and receipt of comments on the Draft Report, our team will prepare the Final TDP
Report. Our team will be on site to present the Final Report to the appropriate governing and advisory
bodies. We will provide 10 full-color bound copies of the report for your internal distribution, as well as
a digital copy so you can print additional copies if you wish and post on the MPO’s website. In addition,
we will provide electronic copies of all background information and data used for and developed during
the project.
Task 10 Deliverables:
 Draft Report (10 hard copies, digital PDF copy)
 Final Report (10 hard copies, digital PDF copy, electronic supporting files) and presentation to
appropriate governing and advisory bodies.
